// A provide method that RESTATES the return type its service already declares
// (`fn size() -> Int`), next to the two spellings that omit it.
//
// The reference parses the restatement and hands it to the type layer, where a
// mismatch against the service op is decided (`examples/rejections/
// t7_provide_param_annotation_mismatch.rvl` is the parameter twin, a T1). The
// self-host admission gate's `p_prov_methods` read the `{`/`=` straight off the
// end of the parameter list, so an annotated method failed the WHOLE component
// with `BAD|bad provide block in component <C>`. That misfire had two costs and
// only the first was visible: it FALSELY REJECTED legal programs, and — worse,
// because it reads as agreement — it MASKED the component's real verdict, so a
// program the reference refuses with a G4 came back tagged BAD from a parse
// stage that never reached the guarantee.
//
// Every method below is inside the covered emitter surface, so this document
// runs through the same unmarked header/body/emitter-readiness projections as
// every other component document rather than being pinned as a special case.
//
// The negative controls are the neighbours, not a separate file:
//   * `size()` carries the annotation in the BRACE-BODY form;
//   * `label(n) -> Str` carries it in the ARROW-SHORTHAND form (the parser has
//     two arms and only one was fixed by stepping over `->` in one of them);
//   * `bump(n)` and `flag()` omit it entirely, in both forms, so the step over
//     the annotation must be conditional — a step taken unconditionally eats the
//     `{` or `=` of an unannotated method and reproduces the same failure from
//     the other side.
service Counter {
  fn size() -> Int
  fn bump(n: Int) -> Int
  fn label(n: Int) -> Str
  fn flag() -> Bool
}

component Tally provides counter: Counter {
  config {
    start: Int = 7
  }

  provide counter {
    fn size() -> Int {
      return config.start
    }
    fn bump(n) {
      return n + 1
    }
    fn label(n) -> Str = n.to_str()
    fn flag() = true
  }
}
